BT /F1 7 Tf 8.4 TL 28 812 Td (Objectives) Tj T* (From August 1st to 20th, 2003, the mean maximum temperature in France exceeded the seasonal norm by 11 to 12°C on nine consecutive days. A major incre) Tj T* (  ase in mortality was then observed, which main epidemiological features are described herein.) Tj T* (Methods) Tj T* (The number of deaths observed from August to November, 2003 in France was compared to those expected on the basis of the mortality rates observed from) Tj T* (   2000 to 2002 and the 2003 population estimates.) Tj T* (Results) Tj T* (From August 1st to 20th, 2003, 15000 excess deaths were observed. From 35 years age, the excess mortality was marked and increased with age. It was 15) Tj T* (  % higher in women than in men of comparable age as of age 45 years. Excess mortality at home and in retirement institutions was greater than that in) Tj T* (   hospitals. The mortality of widowed, single and divorced subjects was greater than that of married people. Deaths directly related to heat, heatstr) Tj T* (  oke, hyperthermia and dehydration increased massively. Cardiovascular diseases, ill-defined morbid disorders, respiratory diseases and nervous syste) Tj T* (  m diseases also markedly contributed to the excess mortality. The geographic variations in mortality showed a clear age-dependent relationship with ) Tj T* (  the number of very hot days. No harvesting effect was observed.) Tj T* (Conclusions) Tj T* (Heat waves must be considered as a threat to European populations living in climates that are currently temperate. While the elderly and people living) Tj T* (   alone are particularly vulnerable to heat waves, no segment of the population may be considered protected from the risks associated with heat waves) Tj T* (  .) Tj T* (Keywords: Adolescent; Adult; Age Factors; Aged; Aged, 80 and over; Cause of Death; Child; Child, Preschool; Female; France; epidemiology; Heat; advers) Tj T* (  e effects; Heat Stress Disorders; mortality; Humans; Infant; Male; Marital Status; Middle Aged; Poisson Distribution; Seasons; Sex Factors) Tj T* (Keywords: Heat wave, Mortality, Modifying Factors, Causes of death, Harvesting) Tj T* (INTRODUCTION) Tj T* (From August 1st to 5th, 2003, the average maximum temperatures recorded in France increased from a value close to the normal value \(25°C\) to 37°C, the) Tj T* (  n remained between 36 and 37°C until August 13th, before beginning to fall \(28°C on August 16th\). Almost all of the population of France, i.e. appro) Tj T* (  ximately 60 million people, was exposed to the heat wave: the temperature exceeded 35°C for at least 9 days in 61 of the 96 French departements. A f) Tj T* (  ew days after the beginning of the heat wave, information reported by the medical emergency services, fire department and hospital emergency departm) Tj T* (  ents showed an abrupt increase in the number of emergency interventions and in the mortality accompanying the heat wave.) Tj T* (The mortality associated with heat has been described and reviewed by several authors. [3,4]) Tj T* (Some authors have described the association between the usual daily fluctuations in temperature and mortality using time series or a spatial approach.) Tj T* (   [6,9,10,26,28,31,33,37,40,44,46,47]) Tj T* (Others have focused on individual major heat episodes, in which an exceptional increase in mortality was observed concomitantly with an exceptional he) Tj T* (  at wave. Those authors described the characteristics of the mortality during or after the heat wave. [1,6,9,11,12,19,25,28,35,38,39,40,42,48] Studie) Tj T* (  s of the 2003 heat waves in Western Europe have been conducted for Portugal,[36] Spain,[45] Italy,[8] England and Wales,[24] the Netherlands,[13] an) Tj T* (  d Switzerland.[15] The probable increase in the frequency of such events in the 21st century has been stressed, as have the time-and place-dependenc) Tj T* (  e of the main characteristics of that mortality.) Tj T* (Given that context and using the latter approach, the epidemiological characteristics of the excess mortality associated with the heat wave in France ) Tj T* (  in August 2003 have been reported herein, on the basis of the studies carried out at the French National Institute for Health and Medical Research \() Tj T* (  Inserm\) by the authors and publicly reported to the French health authorities. [17,18]) Tj T* (MATERIAL AND METHODS) Tj T* (The observations made from August 1st to November 30th, 2003 in mainland France, were compared to the similar observations for the period 2000–2002, t) Tj T* (  aken as the reference period. The mortality data were derived from the merged data bases of Inserm and INSEE \(National Institute of Statistics and E) Tj T* (  conomic Studies\), thus ensuring that the data were complete.) Tj T* (A prior descriptive analysis of the daily mortality-rate process over the reference period has shown that, when the seasonal effect and linear annual ) Tj T* (  trend were controlled, the residuals were distributed as a first-order autoregressive process with extra-Poisson variability. Two methods were then ) Tj T* (  used to estimate the expected number of deaths and the expected variance of daily death counts in 2003. Method A combined modelling of the time cour) Tj T* (  se of monthly mortality rates, by age and gender, from 2000 to 2002, extrapolated to 2003, with estimations of the populations by year, age and gend) Tj T* (  er, from 2000 to 2003. The model adopted was a log-linear Poisson model incorporating a linear annual trend and a specific term for each month of th) Tj T* (  e year. Method B consisted in estimating the mean mortality rates by age, gender and month, from 2000 to 2002, and applying them to the 2003 populat) Tj T* (  ion estimates. In both cases, an over-dispersion parameter was estimated and applied to parameter variance estimation. Ten-year age groups were cons) Tj T* (  idered.) Tj T* (For France as a whole, method A was adopted, since the daily numbers of deaths observed from March to June 2003 were, on average, closer to the estima) Tj T* (  ted values calculated using method A, did not differ significantly from them, and differed significantly from the estimated values calculated using ) Tj T* (  method B. At the finer geographical scale of the 22 “regions” and 96 “departements” that constitute mainland France, method B proved superior to met) Tj T* (  hod A on the basis of the same criteria.) Tj T* (Given the observed \(O\) and the expected \(E\) numbers of deaths, mortality was quantified using excess mortality \(O-E\), rounded up to the nearest intege) Tj T* (  r, and mortality ratios \(O/E\). On the basis of the fluctuations in the daily numbers of deaths that were observed during the reference period \(days ) Tj T* (  of August in 2000, 2001 and 2002\), “95% fluctuation intervals” were defined as intervals in which there was a 95% probability of observing the daily) Tj T* (   numbers of deaths in the future if they had the same expected value, variance and auto-correlation as the deaths which were observed during the ref) Tj T* (  erence period. “Fluctuation intervals” are therefore prediction intervals.) Tj T* (The 95% fluctuation intervals of the observed mortality around the expected mortality for the period 2000–2002 were estimated by day, ten-day period a) Tj T* (  nd month, taking into account the autoregressive structure of the daily mortality-rate process and the extra-Poisson variability observed over the r) Tj T* (  eference period.) Tj T* (The analysis of the causes of death was conducted on the causes of death reported by the physicians making out the death certificates as the “initial”) Tj T* (   cause. The latter is defined as the cause initiating the morbid process resulting in death. The causes of death were classified using the Internati) Tj T* (  onal Classification of Diseases \(ICD10\) and an additional category for deaths reported to be directly related to the heat wave by the physician, i.e) Tj T* (  .: dehydration, hyperthermia and heatstroke.) Tj T* (Usually, coding the medical causes of death involves a long validation process. Validation is still ongoing for 2002. In the 2003 heat wave context, v) Tj T* (  alidation was accelerated for August 2003. It was thus possible to compare the number of deaths by cause occurring in France in the first 20 days of) Tj T* (   August 2003 with the means of the same death counts for August 2000 and 2001, considered to be the expected number of deaths by cause. The statisti) Tj T* (  cal significance of the difference between the observed and expected number of deaths due to a given cause was determined under the hypothesis that ) Tj T* (  the numbers of deaths used in the comparisons were independently Poisson distributed.) Tj T* (The daily maximum and minimum temperature statistics used are those derived from the Météo-France network of 192 meteorological stations representativ) Tj T* (  e of the cities of France. For each of the 96 departements of France, a “number of very hot days” was determined. The number was defined as the numb) Tj T* (  er of days on which the minimum and maximum temperatures reported by the departement‘s meteorological stations simultaneously exceeded the correspon) Tj T* (  ding 30-year averages by 5 and 9°C, respectively, between August 1st and 20th, 2003. The temperature cut-off points \(5 and 9°C\) were chosen so as to) Tj T* (   minimize the deviance of the relationship between the mortality ratios and the number of very hot days using a Poisson regression. The 96 France de) Tj T* (  partements were divided into four groups with nearly equal numbers of expected deaths and an increasing number of very hot days: 0 to 1 day; 2 to 3 ) Tj T* (  days; 4 to 7 days; 8 to 13 days.) Tj T* (RESULTS) Tj T* (Time course of the heat wave and excess mortality) Tj T* (For the period from March to June 2003, approximately 95% of the observed mortality ratios were shown to lie within the 95% fluctuation intervals. Thi) Tj T* (  s was also true for the daily time course of the mortality in July \(figure 1\).) Tj T* (Figure 1. Number of excess deaths observed during the French heat wave from July to September 2003 and average daily maximum \(Tmax\) and minimum \(Tmin\)) Tj T* (   temperatures recorded during the period.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (The X axis shows the 92 days from July 1st to September 30th 2003. The histogram shows the numerical values of the daily excess mortality for August 4) Tj T* (  , 8, 12 and 16.) Tj T* (The dotted horizontal lines show the limits of the 95% fluctuation intervals of the daily number of deaths.) Tj T* (In August, the mortality first deviated significantly from its usual range of fluctuation on the 4th. The daily excess then increased regularly and ma) Tj T* (  ssively, reaching 1193 deaths on August 8th and 2200 deaths on August 12th \(figure 1\). Excess mortality then fell to 1943 deaths on August 13th and ) Tj T* (  988 on August 14th. Mortality had returned to normal as of August 19th. The fall-off in excess mortality reflected the fall in temperature, stabiliz) Tj T* (  ing around August 20th. The cumulative excess mortality from August 1st to 20th was 14 729 deaths, equivalent to an excess mortality of 55%.) Tj T* (Excess mortality by age and gender) Tj T* (Overall, no mortality ratio significantly greater than unity was observed for subjects aged less than 35 years \(table 1\). From the age of 35 years, th) Tj T* (  e mortality ratios were significantly greater than 1 and increased regularly with age, rising from 1.19 for subjects aged 35 to 44 years to 2.00 for) Tj T* (   subjects aged 95 years and over. The number of excess deaths increased very rapidly with age: 11 731 of the 14 729 excess deaths occurred in subjec) Tj T* ET
