BT /F1 7 Tf 8.4 TL 28 812 Td (  culoskeletal diseases increased. These increases were observed for men only. For subjects aged 45–74 years and those aged 75 years or more, the caus) Tj T* (  e-of-death profiles were similar and also similar to that for the total population.) Tj T* (Geographic variability of the excess mortality) Tj T* (In all the 22 regions of France a significant increase in mortality was observed from August 1st to 20th, 2003. However, the geographic heterogeneity ) Tj T* (  of the mortality was marked \(figure 2\). The highest excess mortality was observed in the Paris region \(+142%\), the most heavily populated and urbani) Tj T* (  zed area: +149% for the city of Paris and +174% for the nearest suburb \(immediately surrounding Paris\). The region contiguous with the Paris area in) Tj T* (   the south-west had the second highest mortality ratio \(+104%\), although it has no major cities and is relatively little urbanized. In contrast, low) Tj T* (  er excess mortalities were observed in the Mediterranean, Atlantic and Channel coastal regions \(figure 2 – left map\). While the increases were stati) Tj T* (  stically significant, they were smaller, of the order of 20%.) Tj T* (Figure 2. Geographic distributions of mortality ratios O/E \(left map\) and the number of “very hot days” \(right map\) by Departement in France - August ) Tj T* (  1st to 20th, 2003.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (O: observed number of deaths; E: expected number of deaths; O/E: mortality ratio. The mortality ratios have been grouped into five classes containing ) Tj T* (  approximately equal expected numbers of deaths.) Tj T* (The 96 French departements have been grouped into four classes on the basis of the number of “very hot days”. Number of “very hot days”: number of day) Tj T* (  s on which the minimum and maximum temperatures simultaneously exceeded the corresponding 30-year averages by 5 and 9°C, respectively, between Augus) Tj T* (  t 1st and 20th, 2003.) Tj T* (The mortality ratios observed over the period from August 1st to 20th in the 96 French departements increased with the number of very hot days in each) Tj T* (   departement \(table 2\). The increase was statistically significant for subjects aged less than 55 years, significant and very marked for subjects ag) Tj T* (  ed 55 to 74 years, significant and still more pronounced for subjects aged 75 years and older.) Tj T* (The geographic distribution of the number of “very hot days” \(figure 2 – right map\) was similar to that of mortality ratios \(figure 2 – left map\).) Tj T* (With regard to infants aged less than 1 year, no excess mortality was observed for girls, either in the Paris region or in the rest of France. For boy) Tj T* (  s, the excess mortality in the Paris region \(+55%\) was significantly greater than that in the remainder of France \(+12%\).) Tj T* (Post-heat wave time course of overall mortality) Tj T* (The daily mortality observed from August 19th to September 30th, 2003, for the overall population did not deviate from its usual fluctuation intervals) Tj T* (   \(figure 1\). Such was also the case for the daily mortality observed from October 1st to November 30th, 2003, for the overall population.) Tj T* (The return of mortality to normal was also observed on the ten-day period scale as of the third ten-day period in August, and, on the one-month scale,) Tj T* (   as of the month of September.) Tj T* (Normalization affected both the overall population of France and the various sub-populations that exhibited various degrees of excess mortality over t) Tj T* (  he first two ten-day periods in August. Mortality returned to normal as of August 19th and remained so until November 30th, 2003:) Tj T* (- for all age groups,) Tj T* (- for both men and women,) Tj T* (- for all place-of-death categories \(at home, hospital, institutions and retirement homes\),) Tj T* (- for all matrimonial status categories \(single, divorced, widowed and married\),) Tj T* (- for each of the 22 French regions and each of the 4 groups of departements defined by the number of very hot days experienced between August 1st and) Tj T* (   20th, 2003.) Tj T* (DISCUSSION) Tj T* (In the first fortnight of August 2003, the entire population of the 60 million inhabitants of France was exposed to a heat wave of unprecedented ampli) Tj T* (  tude. The first significant excess mortality was observed 3 days after the maximum temperatures rose above the seasonal normal value. Over the 9 con) Tj T* (  secutive days that maximum temperatures remained 11 to 12°C higher than the seasonal average, the daily excess mortality increased constantly. Morta) Tj T* (  lity returned to normal for the overall population of France on August 19th, 4 days after the maximum temperatures had returned to values close to t) Tj T* (  he seasonal averages. Almost 15 000 excess deaths occurred between August 1st and 20th, 2003, i.e. 55% more than the expected mortality.) Tj T* (On a larger scale, from June to August 2003, the whole of Western Europe was struck by a series of heat waves. Excess mortality was observed in Portug) Tj T* (  al \(+1316 deaths\),[36] Spain \(+6595 to 8648 deaths\),[45] Italy \(in 21 cities: +3134 deaths\),[8] England and Wales \(+2139 deaths\),[24] the Netherland) Tj T* (  s \(+1400 to 2200 deaths\),[13] and Switzerland \(+975 deaths\).[15] Therefore, the overall excess death count of the 2003 heat wave is higher if all We) Tj T* (  stern European countries are considered from June to August. A large fraction of the excess deaths appears to have occurred in France between August) Tj T* (   1st and 20th, with a marked increase in mortality applied to a large population \(60 million people\).) Tj T* (Numerous other episodes of major excess mortality associated with heat waves have been reported in North America,[1,6,11,19,25,35,42,48] Europe,[9,12,) Tj T* (  28,39,40] and Japan.[38] As was the case in those reported heat waves, the excess mortality observed in France in August 2003 increased with age.[1,) Tj T* (  2,37,42,48] It is, however, noteworthy that even though the majority of the excess mortality affected subjects aged 75 years and over \(+11 731 death) Tj T* (  s\), very marked excesses were nonetheless observed in all age groups from 35 to 74 years \(+2930 deaths\). Moderate but significant excess mortality a) Tj T* (  lso occurred among male infants aged less than 1 year \(+25 deaths\). If the elderly are a particularly vulnerable population, the belief that they on) Tj T* (  ly are at risk of heat-related death would thus be erroneous.) Tj T* (Both women,[34,39] and men,[48] have been reported to have higher mortality ratios than the opposite gender. In this study, the overall excess mortali) Tj T* (  ty for women was 75% higher than that for men. However, most of this difference may be explained by the greater longevity of women. When equal ages ) Tj T* (  are considered, from age 55 years, there is only a female excess mortality of about 15%. While the difference needs to be elucidated, it would be im) Tj T* (  prudent to consider that only women are exposed to the risk of death in heat waves.) Tj T* (While the relative increase in mortality observed in hospitals and clinics was less than that for subjects at home or in retirement homes, the absolut) Tj T* (  e excess mortality in the public hospitals was nonetheless major. Thus, all the medical emergency services and emergency care management systems wer) Tj T* (  e subject to exceptionally intense demand. The particularly high mortality ratios observed among people living at home and the substantially higher ) Tj T* (  mortality ratios observed for single and divorced subjects compared to married subjects point to the key role of isolation in vulnerability to heat ) Tj T* (  waves.[29,35,43] People living alone at home are to be considered a sub-population exposed to a particularly high risk.) Tj T* (The deaths occurring during the heat waves described in the literature were related to a wide variety of diseases. [1,11,19,34,39,42,43] Among the var) Tj T* (  iety of medical causes of the excess deaths in France in August 2003, some reflect the very morbid process which resulted in death. This is the case) Tj T* (   for the deaths due to heatstroke, hyperthermia and dehydration, but also, probably, for a fraction, at least, of the deaths for other diseases, par) Tj T* (  ticularly circulatory and respiratory diseases.[1,11,19,20,34,39,42] Certain medical causes of death may also reflect special vulnerability to a hea) Tj T* (  t wave associated with a chronic disease. This was particularly the case for deaths related to mental disorders, nervous system diseases,[2,34,35,43) Tj T* (  ] and endocrine diseases,[34,42] but also, probably, for other causes.) Tj T* (The excess mortality was substantially greater in the departements that had the greatest number of very hot days and more markedly in the older age gr) Tj T* (  oups. However, the relationship is far from perfect: the meteorological indicator of heat-wave exposure used herein is, in fact, only a parameter en) Tj T* (  abling classification of the 96 French departements on an ordinal scale of heat-wave exposure. An analysis of heat heterogeneity on a finer geograph) Tj T* (  ic scale is required. In addition, several heat-wave characteristics appear to have a strong impact on mortality: an abrupt major increase in temper) Tj T* (  ature, whose impact is immediate or very short term;[26] a succession of very hot days;[14,47] and the time during the summer at which the heat wave) Tj T* (   occurs. [10] The exact form of the impact is still far from being elucidated. Heat waves may be described using multiple meteorological indicators ) Tj T* (  such as minimum, maximum or apparent temperature and relative humidity.[12,31,37] The latter remained particularly low during the August 2003 heat w) Tj T* (  ave and the daily meteorological data for that indicator were therefore not taken into account herein.) Tj T* (The excess mortality observed showed great geographic heterogeneity ranging from +20% in the least-affected coastal regions to +142% in the Paris area) Tj T* (   region. The particularly marked excess mortality in the city of Paris and the immediately suburbs is probably related to the higher risks of death ) Tj T* (  in large metropolitan areas reported by certain authors. [6,28] This observation is to be considered in the context of the heat island concept,[6,7,) Tj T* (  32,42,46] and the question of whether there is a synergy between prolonged high temperatures and socioeconomic characteristics.[19,25,30,34,39,47] S) Tj T* (  ome studies also suggest a possible contribution of atmospheric pollution to the increase in the number of deaths during heat waves.[26,28,33,40,44]) Tj T* (   In August 2003, ozone and particulate matter concentrations were particularly high in France: the ozone concentration alert threshold \(240 \(?g/m3\) ) Tj T* (  was exceeded for at least one recording station for 12 consecutive days from August 2nd to 13th, 2003. Moreover, exceptionally high values were obse) Tj T* (  rved in areas where the ozone concentration is generally low. [21]) Tj T* (The above factors could explain some of the differences between the geographic distributions of mortality ratios and the number of “very hot days”. Bo) Tj T* (  th a finer geographic analysis of temperatures and further investigation of the vulnerability factors related to socioeconomic characteristics are r) Tj T* (  equired.) Tj T* (Few observations relating to the existence of a harvesting effect following earlier heat waves are available. They show that a harvesting effect was l) Tj T* (  imited,[5,9,16,26,40] or even non-existent.[20,39] In the present study, no harvesting effect was observed; the excess mortality of the August 2003 ) Tj T* (  heat wave was not followed by a less-than-expected number of deaths until the end of 2003. Moreover, the excess mortality in the first 20 days of Au) Tj T* (  gust 2003 was not followed, from August 21st to November 30th, 2003, by persistent excess mortality.) Tj T* (According to the IPCC’s climate change models, [23] average temperatures should increase by 1.4 to 5.8°C between 1990 and 2100. In 2100, the annual pr) Tj T* (  obability of a summer heat wave similar to that which struck western Europe in August 2003 could be greater than 50%.[41] Given that context, the se) Tj T* (  tting up of systems to prevent the risks related to heat waves, combining long-term preventive measures with alert systems based on short term weath) Tj T* (  er forecasts, needs to be seriously considered even in countries which have, so far, been relatively spared by heat waves. Several preventive and al) Tj T* ET
